Look at this! Let's check the error:
```
[ERROR] file:///app/src/main/kotlin/graphql/kickstart/tools/resolver/FieldResolverScanner.kt:205:82 Only safe (?.) or non-null asserted (!!.) calls are allowed on a nullable receiver of type 'SourceLocation?'.
[ERROR] file:///app/src/main/kotlin/graphql/kickstart/tools/resolver/FieldResolverScanner.kt:205:123 Only safe (?.) or non-null asserted (!!.) calls are allowed on a nullable receiver of type 'SourceLocation?'.
[ERROR] file:///app/src/main/kotlin/graphql/kickstart/tools/resolver/FieldResolverScanner.kt:206:99 Only safe (?.) or non-null asserted (!!.) calls are allowed on a nullable receiver of type 'SourceLocation?'.
```
Line 205-206:
```kotlin
        val sourceName = if (field.sourceLocation != null && field.sourceLocation.sourceName != null) field.sourceLocation.sourceName else "<unknown>"
        val sourceLocation = if (field.sourceLocation != null) "$sourceName:${field.sourceLocation.line}" else "<unknown>"
```
Ah! Why does compilation fail on `field.sourceLocation.sourceName` and `field.sourceLocation.line` when it's inside `if (field.sourceLocation != null)`?
Wait, Kotlin's smart cast flow analysis cannot guarantee that `field.sourceLocation` (which is a getter call `field.getSourceLocation()`) returns the same non-null value on subsequent calls, because it can be an open property or a custom getter in Java/Kotlin that could return a different value or since getter smart-casting is not supported if the property has a custom getter or is a Java class with field nullability.
So Kotlin requires either a local variable assignment to capture the property value (so it can smart cast the local immutable variable), or safe navigation/non-null assertions (`?.` or `!!`).
Capturing in a local variable is extremely clean and idiomatic!
Let's see:
```kotlin
        val location = field.sourceLocation
        val sourceName = if (location != null && location.sourceName != null) location.sourceName else "<unknown>"
        val sourceLocation = if (location != null) "$sourceName:${location.line}" else "<unknown>"
```
Wait, let's verify if `location` as a local immutable variable can be smart-casted. Yes, because `location` is a local `val`, so inside `if (location != null)`, its type is smart-casted to non-null `SourceLocation`.

Let's double-check if there are other places where similar null/smart-cast issues exist.
First, let's edit `RelayConnectionFactory.kt`. Let's read lines 73-85 of `RelayConnectionFactory.kt` to be absolutely precise about matching.
```kotlin
    private fun Directive.forTypeName(): String {
        return (this.getArgument("for").value as StringValue).value
    }

    private fun Directive.withField(field: FieldDefinition): DirectiveWithField {
        return DirectiveWithField(field, this.name, this.arguments, this.sourceLocation, this.comments)
    }

    private fun FieldDefinition.directivesWithField(): List<DirectiveWithField> {
        return this.directives.map { it.withField(this) }
    }

    class DirectiveWithField(val field: FieldDefinition, name: String, arguments: List<Argument>, sourceLocation: SourceLocation, comments: List<Comment>) : Directive(name, arguments, sourceLocation, comments, IgnoredChars.EMPTY, emptyMap()) {
```

Let's edit `RelayConnectionFactory.kt` with exact changes. Let's change:
- `forTypeName()` to use `.value!!` or simply `?.value` if it returns nullable; wait, the return type of `forTypeName()` is non-nullable `String`. So returning `stringValue.value!!` is perfect. Or is there any other value check? No, `getArgument("for").value as StringValue` assumes it is a StringValue. Since in the schema definitions, the directive argument is a non-null string, we can safely do `!!`.
Let's change:
- `Directive.withField` and `DirectiveWithField` constructor to receive `SourceLocation?` instead of `SourceLocation`.
Wait, let's check `comments` parameter in the constructor: `comments: List<Comment>`. Is there any issue with `comments`? No, no compiler error was mentioned for comments. But wait, `comments` might also be nullable? No, the error only mentions: `argument type mismatch: actual type is 'SourceLocation?', but 'SourceLocation' was expected.`
So `sourceLocation` is the only one.

Let's modify `RelayConnectionFactory.kt` using `edit`. Remember to make sure our oldString and newString match exactly! Let's read the exact text and indentation.
